//BUG="a non-void function may fall off the end, returning whatever C left behind"
//ERR any

// `f` returns i32 — deduced from `ret 1` — but only when the `if` is taken.
// Called with -5 it runs off the end of the body, and codegen emits a C
// function with no `return` on that path, so the caller reads whatever happened
// to be in the return register. Today this prints 5, the argument, which is
// simply what was lying around; it is undefined behaviour, not a value.
//
// A function whose return type is not void should be required to return on
// every path, and this is the base case: an `if` with no `else`.
//
// No //MSG on any of the cases 25-32: the diagnostic does not exist yet, so its
// wording cannot be quoted, and the span cannot be predicted either — it might
// land on the function's name or on the point where control leaves the body.
// Add both with the fix.
//
// The cases that must keep compiling are in 05_functions/26-30; an over-strict
// check is as wrong as no check at all.

fn f n
    if n > 0
        ret 1

fn main
    out "{f(-5)}"
